Clusters:
{"cluster_id": "C-0453", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B012-057"], "canonical_claim": "The positive fail-closed CAS GC baseline guard remained untested at HEAD even though the guard survives at `CasGc.cpp:927`; the fresh-pool tests covered only the pass case and a different adopted-seal guard.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-refsnaplog-coverage-audit.md#L127-L142"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0454", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B005-049", "B005-125", "B013-007", "B043-082"], "canonical_claim": "`RunFileReader::next` trusts an un-checksummed `record_count` from the block head and can index beyond the block payload, violating `CasRunFile`'s fail-closed corruption contract. Additionally, `RunFileReader::next()` can read past a block when an unchecksummed `rec_count` is inflated; the fix must cover the whole block header with CRC and use bounds-checked reads with exact-consumption validation. Additionally, Streaming run reads verify per-block and footer CRCs and treat a short or truncated stream as `CORRUPTED_DATA`, never adopting a partial parse. Additionally, `RunFileReader(Backend &, const String & key)` treats an absent run, truncated stream, or CRC failure as `CORRUPTED_DATA`; the borrowed constructor is a zero-copy `std::string_view` over caller-owned bytes.", "issue_ids": ["U#3"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/review1.md#blockers", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-02-cas-gc-snapshot-streaming.md#task-3-runfilereader-borrowed-memory--streaming-modes", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-12-cas-stabilization-cleanup.md#L209-L371", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-02-cas-gc-snapshot-streaming-design.md#runfilereader-two-modes-full-dies", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-12-cas-stabilization-cleanup-design.md#L45-L52"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0455", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B009-089", "B009-096", "B013-011", "B013-012", "B043-089"], "canonical_claim": "The EDGE-BEFORE-OBSERVE invariant in `Build::observeAndAdmit` was guarded only by `chassert(precommitted)`, so release builds could adopt an existing incarnation without durable precommit and watermark protection. Additionally, Adoption of an existing incarnation requires a durable precommit closure, so the Phase A plan adds chassert(precommitted) to both observeAndAdmit overloads; fresh uploads before precommit remain watermark-protected. Additionally, The Phase A ordering guard treats dedup adoption before durable precommit as unsafe because an adopted blob carries the original writer's build_id and is not covered by the newborn-debris watermark.; `Build::observeAndAdmit` must throw `LOGICAL_ERROR` when the ADOPT path is reached with `precommitted == false`; fresh uploads before precommit remain legal because their watermark protection differs from adopted incarnations.; The `EDGE-BEFORE-OBSERVE` precommit requirement must be a release-time check rather than only `chassert(precommitted)`, because `chassert` is compiled out and an early adopt can create a dangle or data loss.", "issue_ids": ["U#12"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/review1.md#major-issues", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-09-cas-writer-gc-simplification-phase-a.md#L121-L175", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-12-cas-stabilization-cleanup.md#L531-L627", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-12-cas-stabilization-cleanup.md#L580-L609", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-09-cas-writer-gc-simplification-design.md##ordering-invariant", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-12-cas-stabilization-cleanup-design.md#L62-L68"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0456", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B013-022"], "canonical_claim": "`dropRefBestEffort` silently swallows rollback failures; a correlated backend outage can leave a permanently live phantom ref because GC reclaims only unreferenced objects.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-12-cas-stabilization-cleanup.md#L1066-L1175", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-12-cas-stabilization-cleanup-design.md#L101-L105"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0457", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-229", "B013-053"], "canonical_claim": "Before consolidation, `RetryStrategy` recognized HTTP 412 while `removeObjectIfTokenMatches` and `copyObjectConditional` used only exception name/message checks, missing stores such as RustFS that leave the exception name unparsed. Additionally, D1 (definitely duplicated): 412/PreconditionFailed detection is reimplemented 3-4 times (`S3::Client::RetryStrategy`, `removeObjectIfTokenMatches`, `copyObjectConditional`) via `getExceptionName()==\"PreconditionFailed\"` or message-string matching; a differently-worded store body could silently misclassify a conditional-write outcome at any one site.", "issue_ids": ["D1"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-12-cas-stabilization-cleanup.md#L2117-L2140", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/00-REPORT.md#L522"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0458", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B014-004"], "canonical_claim": "The `Build::promote` owner-liveness closure guard is a race-only defensive backstop: both legitimate removal paths, abandon and `dropNamespace` cancellation, satisfy `requireAlive` before the closure, while only a narrow promote-versus-`dropNamespace` race reaches the closure guard.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/worklogs/2026-07-12-unattended-refsnaplog-stabilization.md#L74-L80"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0459", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B015-063"], "canonical_claim": "A S38 post-restart finding reported `txn_id 2-0 not strictly greater than greatest applied 2-MAX` after a seal `{2,MAX}`, indicating that sequence zero should not exist.", "issue_ids": ["S38"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/worklogs/2026-07-13-unattended-optimization-round.md#log"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0460", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B015-065"], "canonical_claim": "The `adoptEvidence` relink lifecycle exposure was tracked independently from the abandoned \u00a75 optimization and required a separate investigation.", "issue_ids": ["#42"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/worklogs/2026-07-13-unattended-optimization-round.md#log"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0461", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-368", "B015-076", "B017-076"], "canonical_claim": "The fetch-by-relink residual requires a `stageManifest` stall across at least two GC folds, every replica dropping the part ref, and a deposed leader firing `deleteExact(h,t1)` after promote. Additionally, The original fetch-by-relink correctness hole was that the sender released its part before the receiver committed its ref, leaving no in-degree overlap during a sufficiently long precommit stall; the affected reports were `#42` and `#43`. Additionally, Fetch-by-relink has no retention pin: the sender is fire-and-forget and releases the source part while the receiver's `precommitAdd` edge-PUT can stall, so if the source part goes Outdated and GC completes at least two folds before the edge lands, the sender's blob is reclaimed while the receiver's build still promotes successfully, yielding a committed manifest naming a deleted blob discoverable only by fsck; this is the umbrella review's top blocker (risk score 85).", "issue_ids": ["#42", "#43"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-14-cas-adoptevidence-relink-lifecycle-exposure.md#verdict-plausible-needs-repro-scoped", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-20-umbrella-review-cas-vs-antalya-26.6.md#L15-L20", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-15-cas-fetch-handoff-retention-pin-design.md#motivation"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0462", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B015-135", "B015-137"], "canonical_claim": "The planned \u00a75 spare-heal transition reintroduced the removed `clearSparedMeta` behavior by clearing a tombstone while the blob retained condemn-time token `t1`. Additionally, A spare that clears a tombstone can advertise a blob as Clean at token `t1`, allowing a stale deposed-leader `deleteExact(h,t1)` to delete the live body.", "issue_ids": ["\u00a75"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-14-cas-s5-spare-clear-reopens-dataloss.md#mechanism", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-14-cas-s5-spare-clear-reopens-dataloss.md#summary"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0463", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B015-138"], "canonical_claim": "A token-preserving `adoptEvidence` recovery path can re-reference a condemned blob at token `t1`, invalidating the premise that all in-degree recovery uses fresh-token resurrection.", "issue_ids": ["#42", "\u00a75"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-14-cas-s5-spare-clear-reopens-dataloss.md#mechanism"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0464", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B015-139"], "canonical_claim": "The original `CaMetaAbsenceClean.tla` gate was false-green because `GcSpareHeal` atomically reset `queuedDeletes`, unlike the real code.", "issue_ids": ["\u00a75"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-14-cas-s5-spare-clear-reopens-dataloss.md#false-green"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0465", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B015-144"], "canonical_claim": "The separate adoptEvidence exposure exists because the destination's durable edge is committed after the body-time adopt evidence and the trusted promote path can defer absent-body detection to fsck.", "issue_ids": ["#42"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-14-cas-s5-spare-clear-reopens-dataloss.md#separate-pre-existing-finding-filed-independently"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0466", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B019-002", "B019-018", "B019-026"], "canonical_claim": "The MOVE-to-CA implementation has two independent defects: the part-path parser routes files under `<table>/moving/<part>/` to the literal ref `moving`, and `DataPartStorageOnDiskBase::clonePart` copies files through separate autocommit transactions that a CA disk cannot use for a multi-file part. Additionally, Scenario S36 found that TO-CA MOVE was unimplemented: the generic mover copied files independently, producing `Code 236 promote: ref 'moving' already names a different committed manifest` or `NOT_IMPLEMENTED` for a column file.; Both L1 identity routing and L2 whole-part atomicity are required: L2 alone still collides on ref `moving`, while L1 alone still autocommits per file and can raise `NOT_IMPLEMENTED`.", "issue_ids": ["S36"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-17-cas-move-to-ca.md#L5-L7", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-17-cas-move-to-ca-design.md#L41-L41", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-17-cas-move-to-ca-design.md#L6-L14"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0467", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-131", "B019-031", "B019-043"], "canonical_claim": "Without install-time materialization, the COW overlay grows across successful flushes and later `working = rt->state` copies regress toward copying all rows touched over the object's lifetime. Additionally, An xhigh two-model review found E5's fast path unreachable in production flush: the `working` copy held a second reference to the base, keeping `use_count()` at 2 so the unique-owner fast path never fired and the slow O(N) path always ran; fixed by an xfix round (item A, working-lifetime) making the 1700x speedup real, confirmed by a paired ProfileEvents counter in the follow-up soak.; `RefTableState::committed` is replaced by an ordered COW map so copies in `CasRefLedger::flushRefBatch` and `applyRefLogTxn`/`admits` cost O(touched rows), while behavior, snapshot bytes, admission decisions, and errors remain byte-identical.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-17-cas-reftable-cow-map.md#L1-L7", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-17-cas-reftable-cow-map.md#L1061-L1065", "docs/superpowers/worklogs/2026-07-21-unattended-reftablestate-experiments.md#timeline"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0468", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B019-066", "B019-107", "B033-089"], "canonical_claim": "Review finding \u211610 is a real TSan data race on the plain `std::function` event sink because `setEventSink` runs after `Pool::open` starts the renewal thread, although the traced timing refutes crash-grade overlap. Additionally, The CAS event sink has an unsynchronized assignment/read race because renewal threads can read a shared `std::function` while `setEventSink` assigns it.; The CAS event sink must be installed in `PoolConfig` or `Pool::open` before `mountWritable` starts the renewal thread; `setEventSink` is retained only as a pre-open test hook.", "issue_ids": ["\u211610"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-17-codex-triage-fix-wave.md#L180-L192", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-17-codex-review-triage.md#L228-L237", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-17-codex-review-triage.md#L29-L30", "docs/superpowers/reports/20260717_codex_review.md#high-severity"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0469", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B019-069", "B033-092"], "canonical_claim": "Review finding \u211613 is real hardening: telemetry emitted after durable ref publication must not turn a successful operation into a caller-visible failure; post-durable emission should catch, log, and continue. Additionally, A propagating telemetry exception after durable ref publication can leave transaction state inconsistent: the caller sees failure while a live ref exists and the transaction lacks the ref for success or rollback.", "issue_ids": ["\u211613"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-17-codex-review-triage.md#L32-L33", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-17-codex-review-triage.md#L73-L82", "docs/superpowers/reports/20260717_codex_review.md#high-severity"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0470", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B019-077"], "canonical_claim": "Review finding \u211620 is split: decoder identity fields need hygiene checks, duplicate JSON keys already throw `CORRUPTED_DATA`, and fold seals lack a key-to-generation binding that could corrupt the GC cursor or cause premature deletion.", "issue_ids": ["\u211620"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-17-codex-review-triage.md#L323-L341", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-17-codex-review-triage.md#L40-L41"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0471", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B019-078"], "canonical_claim": "Review finding \u211621 is real: casting a decoded `uint64` version to `uint32` before validation turns `4294967299` into version `3` and can bypass compatibility checks.", "issue_ids": ["\u211621"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-17-codex-review-triage.md#L41-L42", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-17-codex-review-triage.md#L98-L104"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0472", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B019-082", "B019-110"], "canonical_claim": "Review finding \u211625 is latent terminal-transaction hardening: a failed `commit` can leave `PartStaging::published=true`, causing a hypothetical retry on the same transaction object to skip work and report success.; A content-addressed transaction that fails `commit` is terminal: later `commit` or `tryCommit` must throw `LOGICAL_ERROR` instead of retrying the failed object.", "issue_ids": ["\u211625"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-17-codex-triage-fix-wave.md#L194-L208", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-17-codex-review-triage.md#L119-L125", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-17-codex-review-triage.md#L45-L46"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0473", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B019-083"], "canonical_claim": "Review finding \u211626 is a real stale-payload mechanism but deferred because the only production appender is the single-writer, per-mutation-unique `MergeTreeMutationEntry::writeCSN` path.", "issue_ids": ["\u211626"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-17-codex-review-triage.md#L311-L321", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-17-codex-review-triage.md#L46-L47"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0474", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B019-146"], "canonical_claim": "The structural defect is that `MergeTreeData::Transaction` treats disk durability as a nested side effect of its commit, while rollback never calls an undo or abort operation on the disk transaction.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-17-dataloss-traced-root-cause.md#L97-L101"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0475", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B020-005"], "canonical_claim": "Six ASan stack-use-after-scope findings were caused by a reference-capturing event sink writing to a local `events` vector after that vector had been destroyed while the owning `Pool` was still being torn down.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-18-asan-battery-rca.md#L14-L63"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0476", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B020-007"], "canonical_claim": "`CasPoolShutdown.UnresolvedWedgeSkipsFarewell` had a heap-use-after-free because `const Layout & layout = store->layout()` was used after `store.reset()` freed the `Pool` containing the referenced `Layout`.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-18-asan-battery-rca.md#L78-L119"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0477", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B020-048"], "canonical_claim": "The S13 quiesce wedge was associated with a `partAccess` use-after-free and a mutex that blocked `gcHealth` for an entire GC round; the fix split round serialization from pointer publication and was validated by a clean third S13 attempt.", "issue_ids": ["S13"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/worklogs/2026-07-17-unattended-r5-r6-round.md#L421-L425"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0478", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B021-034"], "canonical_claim": "The CAS review triage confirmed a scheduler/`cas_store` shutdown use-after-free race and an event-sink assignment/read data race; task T9 fixed both in commit `846a4f62a62`.", "issue_ids": ["T9"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-19-session-full-summary.md#L10-L21"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0479", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B021-066"], "canonical_claim": "A future mutation path that bypasses `applyOpInPlace` would desynchronize the ref-ledger counters; the debug `chassert` is the immediate detector for this invariant violation.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-20-cas-ref-admits-incremental-budget-design.md#L206-L213"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0480", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-130", "B023-027"], "canonical_claim": "The first `TrustedReplay` validation-elision design failed open on double-owner history; the correction runs the cross-owner check unconditionally and makes snapshot duplicate-owner validation fail closed. Additionally, A two-model adversarial consult found that E1's fail-closed elision (obsoleted by E2 since the ownership check became O(1)) traded away fail-closed behavior on the core invariant: a double-owner input can silently drift the owned-manifest index, and a drifted index lets `LiveAppend` manufacture fresh invalid durable history, risking GC premature delete.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-21-reftablestate-experiments.md#adversarial-consults-fix-t7", "docs/superpowers/worklogs/2026-07-21-unattended-reftablestate-experiments.md#timeline"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0481", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B023-091", "B023-092"], "canonical_claim": "Concurrent part workers exposed a latent `CasRefLedger` defect: a leader throwing before carve could leave its `RefMutationItem` in `rt->pending`, allowing a follower to invoke a dangling by-reference `build_ops` closure.; After any `appendRefOps` return or throw, every `RefMutationItem` must be done or owned by a still-blocked caller; no item may remain pending for a future leader after its caller's closure state is gone.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-22-cas-parallel-write-path.md#task-1-ref-lane-exception-safety-the-bundled-dependency", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-22-cas-parallel-write-path-design.md#bundled-dependency-ref-lane-exception-safety-a-latent-bug-parallelism-activates"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0482", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B024-014", "B024-078"], "canonical_claim": "The former interleaved ref-ledger carve could lose already-popped items when `seen_refs`, batch, or `owned_items` allocation threw, leaving waiters blocked and callback lifetimes unsafe.; The stage-1 carve test seam distinguishes plan allocations (`PlanSeenRefs`, `PlanBatchGrow`, `PlanReserveOwned`) from publish and validation phases, and requires every plan-phase failure to leave already-selected items either retryable or never popped.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-23-cas-writepath-stage1.md#task-7", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-22-cas-writepath-stage1-internal-design.md#two-phase-carve"]}

